<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#gods-wife-of-amun">Glossary</a> · Term · title</p><h1 class="enc-title">God's Wife of Amun</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Divine Adoratrice of Amun</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">God's Wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a> was the title of the highest-ranking priestess of the god Amun at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>. From the Third Intermediate Period it was held by unmarried royal daughters, each adopting her successor, and the office controlled great temple wealth and political influence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a>. Kings secured Thebes by having their daughters adopted, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/psamtik-i">Psamtik I</a> did with Nitocris in 656 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gods-wife-of-amun.jpg" alt="God's Wife of Amun" width="320" height="800" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: tutincommon (John Campana) · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statue_of_Ankhenesneferibre_by_John_Campana.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>A shorter title, God's Wife, was already in use under the Twelfth Dynasty, when it was held by the non-royal women Iy-meret-nebes and Neferu, and as early as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-intermediate-period">First Intermediate Period</a> there is mention of a Wife of the God in connection with the god Min. The full title of God's Wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a> appears only from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eighteenth-dynasty-of-egypt">Eighteenth Dynasty</a>, whose kings came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>, drove the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyksos">Hyksos</a> out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and believed that their local god Amun had guided them to victory.</p><p>The first royal woman to hold the new title was Queen Ahmose-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nefertari">Nefertari</a>, wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmose-i">Ahmose I</a>, as a stele in the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnak">Karnak</a> records. She passed it to her daughter Ahmose-Meritamun, who handed it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatshepsut">Hatshepsut</a> before Hatshepsut took the throne as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a>. Both Ahmose-Nefertari and Hatshepsut sometimes used it in place of the title King's Principal Wife, and Hatshepsut passed it on to her own daughter Neferure.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The title referred to the myth of the king's divine birth, in which his mother was made pregnant by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a>. In practice the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> used the office to secure royal authority over the Theban region and the powerful priesthood of Amun. Scenes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatshepsut">Hatshepsut</a>'s Chapelle Rouge show the God's Wife and a male priest in a rite that seems aimed at destroying the names of enemies, and other scenes show her worshipping the gods, being purified in the sacred lake and following the king into the sanctuary, though they say little about her duties.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thutmose-iii">Thutmose III</a> the title passed among his mother, lesser wives and a daughter, and Amenhotep II greatly reduced the power and prestige of the role. It fell out of favour when Amenhotep IV, renamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhenaten">Akhenaten</a>, raised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aten">Aten</a> above the other gods and suppressed the worship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a>. Horemheb, the last ruler of the dynasty, restored the priesthood of Amun but appointed its priests from the high ranks of his army. Ramesses VI of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/twentieth-dynasty-of-egypt">Twentieth Dynasty</a> revived the office for his daughter Iset, adding the title Divine Adoratrice of Amun.</p><p>The office reached the height of its political power in the late Third Intermediate Period, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shepenupet-i">Shepenupet I</a>, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osorkon-iii">Osorkon III</a>, held it at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>. The Nubian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashta">Kashta</a> had his daughter Amenirdis appointed as her successor, and her tomb at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medinet-habu">Medinet Habu</a> shows the standing of the post. The office lasted until 525 BCE under Ankhnesneferibre, successor of Nitocris, when the Persian Empire overthrew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/psamtik-iii">Psamtik III</a>, the last Saite ruler, and enslaved his daughter. It was not recreated when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> later regained its independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “God's Wife of Amun” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The office kept great prestige, and power at Thebes later passed to the <b>God's Wife of Amun</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/theocracy-of-amun-at-thebes">Theocracy of Amun at Thebes</a></li><li><blockquote>As Assyria weakened he threw off its overlordship, and in 656 BCE he took Thebes peacefully by having his daughter Nitocris adopted as the future <b>God's Wife of Amun</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/twenty-sixth-dynasty-of-egypt">Twenty-sixth Dynasty of Egypt</a></li><li><blockquote>Osorkon III and his son Takelot III ruled as co-regents and placed family members as high priests and as <b>God's Wife of Amun</b> at Thebes.</blockquote><a href="/state/twenty-third-dynasty-of-egypt">Twenty-third Dynasty of Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
